  "title": "Exact colorings of graphs",
  "statement": {
    "representation": "markdown-latex",
    "text": "Conjecture For $c \\geq m \\geq 1$, let $P(c,m)$ be the statement that given any exact $c$-coloring of the edges of a complete countably infinite graph (that is, a coloring with $c$ colors all of which must be used at least once), there exists an exactly $m$-colored countably infinite complete subgraph. Then $P(c,m)$ is true if and only if $m=1$, $m=2$, or $c=m$.",
